WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
What a working engineer would do.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:08.173
Before labeling anything, write the labeling guideline and the schema, and label 30 examples yourself.

3
00:00:08.453 --> 00:00:12.165
You will find the ambiguities (is “someone should” an action item?

4
00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.173
is “by Friday” a due date?)

5
00:00:14.453 --> 00:00:19.552
faster than any process will, and your decisions become the guideline’s worked examples.

6
00:00:19.832 --> 00:00:36.749
Then plan sources on purpose: real inputs for the bulk and the whole test set, expert-written examples for rare and high-stakes cases, model-drafted labels only after the terms question has an answer, and human review for every drafted label that will be used as gold.

7
00:00:37.029 --> 00:00:38.693
Record provenance per example.

8
00:00:38.973 --> 00:00:41.619
Validate the file in code before every run.

9
00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:50.667
And start small: 50 to 100 examples, a training run, a score on the validation set, and only then a decision about labeling more.

10
00:00:50.947 --> 00:00:59.821
Next lesson covers the part that makes the score trustworthy: splits, duplicates, leakage, label noise, and a test set that matches production.
